{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791565327,"nonce":"UOe9XRP7th5a3r8xKTAZi3MHQEk=","title":"Strings at the N-API boundary: atoms, one call per tick, and UTF-16 text","message":"Every string that crosses into the addon today is a napi-rs String: N-API measures it, transcodes it to UTF-8 and the addon allocates it, per call. The host interface puts strings on the hottest path (property names and values, classes, attributes, tags, event types), so they need a cheaper crossing.\n\n**Measured** (2026-10-09, Node 24.2.0, M-series Mac, napi 3.14 release build with fat LTO; a standalone addon whose functions only sum the bytes they get; 200,000 strings per case, strings built at run time; ns per string, two to three runs agreed):\n- An N-API call alone (an integer in and out): about 57 ns.\n- napi-rs String per call: 115-145 for a 17-byte class name or a 25-byte CSS value; 1,200-1,300 for 1 KB of ASCII text; 680-890 for 200 non-ASCII characters.\n- An interned atom id per call: 77-96, whatever the length (the call's own cost).\n- Atom ids for 1,000 strings in one Uint32Array, one call: 14-18 for short strings, including the JavaScript Map lookup from string to id.\n- UTF-16 per call (napi_get_value_string_utf16, no transcoding): about 100 short, 500-590 for 1 KB, 105-120 non-ASCII. Made UTF-8 natively with String::from_utf16_lossy it is far slower (3,000-3,500 for 1 KB); with the simdutf crate, 145 short, 666 for 1 KB, 210 non-ASCII.\n- Latin-1 read (napi_get_value_string_latin1): 139 for 1 KB of ASCII, but it truncates characters above U+00FF silently, and JavaScript cannot cheaply know a string is ASCII.\n- TextEncoder.encodeInto into a shared buffer, 1,000 strings per call: 72-80 short, 770-800 for 1 KB. No better than one call per string.\n\n**Plan**\n1. Names are atoms. CSS property names are fixed numeric ids, a generated table, with no interning call. Tags and event types are fixed numbers too. Class names, attribute names, ids and keyword values are interned once per context; the TypeScript side keeps a Map from string to id and passes the id. The atom table is blinc_abi's (src/css Atoms), shared by the cascade, so a node's class and a selector's class compare as integers.\n2. One call per tick. Host operations go into a command buffer (Uint32Array and Float64Array views on one ArrayBuffer) and one call applies them, so the call's own 57 ns is paid once per tick rather than per operation (d1a4fbe).\n3. Numbers stay numbers. A length crosses as a value and a unit code, not as \"50%\" (layout.rs dimension takes Either\u003cf64, String\u003e today).\n4. Free text (text content, SVG markup, CSS source) crosses as UTF-16 and is made UTF-8 natively with SIMD (simdutf): 1.9 times faster for 1 KB of ASCII, 3.2 times for non-ASCII. Strings under a few dozen characters keep the UTF-8 path, which is cheaper there. Text equal (===) to what a node already holds is not sent again.\n5. Back to JavaScript, names are atom ids, read from a JavaScript-side mirror of the table, and new text is made as Latin-1 when native code knows it is ASCII.\n6. Atoms are for names and keywords, not arbitrary values, so the table stays bounded; it is freed with its context.\n\nThe benchmark addon and script: scratchpad, to be added under benchmarks/ when the command buffer lands, so the gains are measured on the real path.","files":null}]}